  again in vitro and we have this nice  robust angiogenic burst  outside of that so we know that our  vessels not only are there but they're  functional  and again this has implications if we  want to tie this into other types of  tissue models or  perhaps pursue a therapeutic  interestingly  uh while baseline adipocyte functions  such as lipolysis  is good but not really affected by the  vessels that are present  the vessels in themselves induce the  adipocytes to upregulate the insulin  receptor which obviously is important in  not only glucose metabolism and fat  function but  sets the stage for an insulin resistance  modeling  so again here we see this nice dynamic  response  of the of the adipose organoids to  certain challenges  as well as some new features when we  again start to reconstitute that tissue  environment more  with our microvasculature  in all cases we've been exploring the  utility of  through tissue perfusion uh in this  particular example it's with our thick  liver construct  using our uh our bioassembly platform  we're 3d printing up sacrificial  networks  and assembling our thick tissue around  those channels  and then using a an office or a  customized  system that works with off-the-shelf  plastic such as multi-well plates  uh that we've developed in-house we can  really start to provide perfusion  through that tissue bed  where the channels themselves are formed  by the tissue and so  this gives us as added flexibility we're  certainly seeing the  changes that perfusion introduces not  only just in survival but in some  functional differences  as well and so um i'll speak a little  bit later about  complexity but our particular approach  has been designed to give flexibility on  on how you assemble the model depending  on  what types of questions you want to  answer and what your objectives are  finally while originally i  talked to you about how we can get our  micro vessels to form a neovasculture in  vitro  you can take that and plant it in vivo  say under the skin of a  immune compromised mouse and have that  connect inoculate to the whole  circulation and drive  the formation of a new microcirculation  but we wanted to recapitulate that full  process  in vitro and so um what i'm showing you  here is our  in vitro human microcirculation we're  getting ready to assemble this and put  this  in of to review for publication  but we're combining our microvessel  technology  uh brand name angiomix  with a microfluidic channel system such  that we have and then the upper right  perhaps is the best  schematic of that where we have  microfluidic channels that we  then grow uh adjacent  a neovasculture from our isolated micro  vessels similar what i've been talking  about so far  and inducing them to to connect or  inosculate with the channel walls that  have been aligned with endothelial cells  and form a network and then start to via  intravascular perfusion and controlling  hemodynamic cues  drive and mature that microcirculation  and so on the images here on the left  the upper left  corner is is our endothelial saw line  channel  on the lower left is a neovasculature  that's grown up here's shown in red  fluorescence  in the middle are phase images where the  two arrows  open arrows highlight the walls of the  channel and and adjacent to that  is a is a network of micro vessels that  are formed the arrows are pointing to  some and not certainly not all  the next adjacent panel is showing that  inosculation event between  our microvessel from that network to  that channel wall and the right are  stills from a video  where we've introduced beads into the  channels and then we're observing those  beads  moving in through the different vessel  elements of that micro circulation  and so we're like i mentioned with the  process of finalizing this body of work  putting it out there for review and then  uh  getting input on what people think  obviously there's a lot of implications  both  both in therapeutics but now also we're  starting to think  how can we combine our other previous  models such as our adipocyte model  with the islet model and start to really  create  a more complex physiological system  where each of these model systems are  connected via bonafide microcirculation  and really start to recapitulate as much  of that in body  tissue environment and physiology as we  possibly can  so with that uh summarize a little bit  what i've been talking about here  with you one is  so you know the idea of recapitulating a  tissue in vitro  uh recognizing full well that those  tissues are complex and varied  um typically when you start combining  different components into the  into a tissue-like environment you start  to see emergent behaviors you start to  see  kind of a greater than the sum of the  parts type behavior  and that's primarily due to that high  integration and dynamics so our  our modeling strategy is such that we  enable those dynamics to play themselves  out  uh of course you know we emphasize uh we  have expertise in this uh  that of course then a bias in the need  to have a micro vasculature in the  tissue it's  we think it's a critical element with or  without perfusion we're seeing evidence  that the micro vessels present  contribute to good tissue model behavior  we also know that the vasculature  participates in the disease  process we've not explored much of that  yet we're just starting to do that now  that these models are in place  uh and and we've gained the appreciation  that they're  they really serve the microvascular and  those elements of the microvasculature  serve as a  reservoir for stromal cells in  particular we're paying attention to  tissue resident macrophages  we think that's key and all of that  really is starting to  realize that microvessel parenchyma  cooperativity  that we just weren't seeing with  endothelial cells alone some cases we  were but not in all  and so we're really pleased that we're  able to  advance this 